 Let $z = 2e^{i\theta}.$  Then $z^3 = 8e^{i3\theta}$ and $z^5 = 32e^{i5\theta}.$  The distance between $(3 + 4i)z^3$ and $z^5$ is
\begin{align*}
|(3 + 4i)z^3 - z^5| &= |(3 + 4i)8e^{i3\theta} - 32e^{i5\theta}| \\
&= |24e^{i3\theta} + 32ie^{i3\theta} - 32e^{i5\theta}| \\
&= |24\cos 3\theta + 32i\cos 3\theta - 32\cos 5\theta - 32i\sin 5\theta| \\
&= \sqrt{(24\cos 3\theta - 32\cos 5\theta)^2 + (32\cos 3\theta - 32\sin 5\theta)^2}.
\end{align*}
Using the identity $\cos 5\theta = 16\cos^5 \theta - 20\cos^3 \theta + 5\cos \theta$ and $\sin 5\theta = 5\sin \theta - 20\sin^3 \theta + 16\sin^5 \theta$, we can rewrite this as
\begin{align*}
|(3 + 4i)z^3 - z^5| &= \sqrt{(24\cos 3\theta - 32(16\cos^5 \theta - 20\cos^3 \theta + 5\cos \theta))^2 + (32\cos 3\theta - 32(5\sin \theta - 20\sin^3 \theta + 16\sin^5 \theta))^2} \\
&= \sqrt{10240\cos^7 \theta - 12288\cos^5 \theta + 4608\cos^3 \theta - 2048\cos \theta}.
\end{align*}
To maximize this expression, we need to find the maximum value of the function $f(x) = 10240x^7 - 12288x^5 + 4608x^3 - 2048x$ on the interval $[-1, 1].$  Taking the derivative, we get
\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= 71680x^6 - 61440x^4 + 13824x^2 - 2048 \\
&= 1024(x^2 - 1)^2(x^2 + 1)^2.
\end{align*}
Since $f'(x) = 0$ only at $x = \pm 1,$ the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-1, 1]$ is $f(1) = 0.$  Therefore, the largest possible distance between $(3 + 4i)z^3$ and $z^5$ is $\boxed{14}.$ </think> <answer> $\boxed{14}$ </answer>

