 First, we need to find the direction vector of the line. The direction vector is the difference between the vector at $t = 0$ and the vector at $t = 1$. From the given information, we can determine the vector at $t = 0$ and $t = 1$. Since the vector at $t = -1$ is $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 8 \end{pmatrix}$ and the vector at $t = 2$ is $\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -2 \\ -4 \end{pmatrix}$, we can find the vector at $t = 0$ by subtracting $3$ times the vector at $t = -1$ from the vector at $t = 2$. \[
\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -2 \\ -4 \end{pmatrix} = 3 \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 8 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix} \implies \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ -11 \\ -28 \end{pmatrix}
\] Now, we can find the vector at $t = 1$ by adding the direction vector to the vector at $t = 0$. \[
\begin{pmatrix} -3 + (-3) \\ -11 + (-11) \\ -28 + (-28) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -6 \\ -22 \\ -56 \end{pmatrix}
\] The direction vector is \[
\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\ -22 \\ -56 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 8 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -7 \\ -25 \\ -64 \end{pmatrix}
\] Next, we need to find the vector at $t = 3$. We can find it by adding $4$ times the direction vector to the vector at $t = 0$. \[
